## Verify the setup

```bash
{% if sdd_framework == 'spec-kit' -%}
specify check                  # validates Python, uv, git, installed agents
ls .specify/                   # memory/, specs/, scripts/, templates/
{%- else -%}
openspec --version             # CLI on PATH
ls openspec/                   # AGENTS.md, project.md, specs/, changes/
{%- endif %}
```

Smoke-test in your agent session: `{{ '/speckit-constitution' if sdd_framework == 'spec-kit' else '/opsx:propose dummy-change' }}`.
If the slash command is recognised, the setup is wired correctly. For
OpenSpec, clean up afterwards with `rm -rf openspec/changes/dummy-change`.

## SDD and the workflow skills

The nine workflow skills in `skills/` stay relevant — SDD does not replace
them, it deepens the ones that matter most:

| Workflow skill | With {{ 'Spec-Kit' if sdd_framework == 'spec-kit' else 'OpenSpec' }} |
|---|---|
| `/spec` | Becomes {{ '`/speckit-specify` + `/speckit-clarify`' if sdd_framework == 'spec-kit' else '`/opsx:propose`' }} — a multi-artifact contract instead of one file |
| `/plan` | {{ 'Becomes `/speckit-plan` + `/speckit-tasks`' if sdd_framework == 'spec-kit' else 'Lives inside the proposal — no separate skill needed' }} |
| `/test` | Driven from the spec's acceptance criteria as before |
| `/delegate` | Bundle the spec artefacts directly into the brief |
| `/review` | Verify the diff against {{ '`spec.md` + `tasks.md`' if sdd_framework == 'spec-kit' else 'the proposal + delta specs' }} |

## Anti-patterns

- **Verbose specs for trivial tasks.** A 16-criterion spec for a one-line
  bugfix is cargo-cult. Skip SDD for those.
- **Set-and-forget specs.** On multi-week initiatives, re-align the spec to
  reality after each iteration. Specs that drift become misleading docs.
- **Mixing both frameworks.** One repo, one tool. Pick at setup time.

